cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this project or its findings, please cite it as below."
title: "testward: How Often Do Old Tests Fail on Merged Agent Pull Requests, and Does It Matter?"
authors:
  - family-names: "Syed"
    given-names: "Zaid Ali"
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0003-4313-1510"
version: 0.1.0
date-released: 2026-11-14
url: "https://github.com/zaidwhy/testward"
repository-code: "https://github.com/zaidwhy/testward"
license: MIT
abstract: >
  A pre-registered kill test for a merge gate that replays a pull request's base-branch tests
  against the merged code. Across 447 merged agent-authored pull requests from the AIDev
  dataset, old tests fail on the merged code in 62 of 170 conclusive replays (36.5 percent).
  Two raters and read-only history tracing found 59 of the 62 to be intended behaviour changes
  and 3 to be real undisclosed regressions, two of them stale branches merged over newer work.
  No agent was found weakening a test to hide its own failure. The gate is killed by its own
  pre-registered rule.
keywords:
  - coding agents
  - pull requests
  - regression testing
  - code review
  - pre-registration
  - inter-rater agreement
